From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PATCH] tcp: register Lab126's /proc/tcpreset once

The stock kernel's own defect, and it lands in the diagnostic this project
asks readers to send.

`/proc/tcpreset` is Lab126's: writing "42" or "43" plus a list of ports to
skip resets this machine's IPv4 TCP connections (proc_tcpreset_write, under
CONFIG_LAB126). It is created inside tcp_proc_register(), which is called
once per address family -- tcp4_proc_init() at net/ipv4/tcp_ipv4.c and
tcp6_proc_init() at net/ipv6/tcp_ipv6.c -- so the second call creates an
entry that already exists, and proc_register() answers with a WARN plus a
full backtrace. It is the first line of every boot's kernel log:

    proc_dir_entry '/proc/tcpreset' already registered
    Modules linked in:
    Backtrace:
    [<c003d5cc>] (dump_backtrace+0x0/0x10c) from [<c04a8fd0>] (dump_stack+0x18/0x1c)
    ...
    [<c0167984>] (proc_register+0x0/0x174) from [<c016840c>] (create_proc_entry+0x60/0xa0)
    [<c01683ac>] (create_proc_entry+0x0/0xa0) from [<c0406578>] (tcp_proc_register+0xa0/0xd8)
    [<c04064d8>] (tcp_proc_register+0x0/0xd8) from [<c046db2c>] (tcp6_proc_init+0x18/0x1c)

Nothing breaks: the second registration fails, the entry still exists once
and still works, and it is an IPv4 facility that IPv6 has no use for. Two
reasons to fix it anyway, and the second is the one that matters:

  * the kernel ring is 32 KB (CONFIG_LOG_BUF_SHIFT=15) and this spends about
    1.5 KB of it at 0.47 seconds, which is the part of the ring a panic
    capture needs most;
  * the entry's log filter keeps WARNING, Backtrace: and register dumps on
    purpose (boot/launcher/userlog.c), so those twenty-odd lines go into
    every android/logs/<time>/ folder a reader hands on, at the top, where
    they read as this port's own backtrace.

The guard is the one proc_register() itself would want: create the entry if
it is not there. tcp_proc_unregister() already tests the same pointer before
removing, so this only makes the two sides agree -- and it leaves the
existing behaviour alone, because the pointer is NULL until the first call.

--- a/net/ipv4/tcp_ipv4.c
+++ b/net/ipv4/tcp_ipv4.c
@@ -2515,11 +2515,16 @@ int tcp_proc_register(struct net *net, struct tcp_seq_afinfo *afinfo)
 		rc = -ENOMEM;

 #if defined(CONFIG_LAB126)
-        proc_tcpreset = create_proc_entry(N_PROC_TCPRESET, S_IWUGO, NULL);
-        if (proc_tcpreset != NULL) {
-            proc_tcpreset->data = NULL;
-            proc_tcpreset->read_proc = NULL;
-            proc_tcpreset->write_proc = proc_tcpreset_write;
-        }
+        /* Called once per address family, and the entry is not per-net (its
+         * parent is NULL), so the second family would re-create a name that is
+         * already there and take a WARN with a backtrace for it. */
+        if (proc_tcpreset == NULL) {
+            proc_tcpreset = create_proc_entry(N_PROC_TCPRESET, S_IWUGO, NULL);
+            if (proc_tcpreset != NULL) {
+                proc_tcpreset->data = NULL;
+                proc_tcpreset->read_proc = NULL;
+                proc_tcpreset->write_proc = proc_tcpreset_write;
+            }
+        }
 #endif

 	return rc;
